WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:03.280
Film Courage:&nbsp; Markus, are you leaning more
toward television….in your writing?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.910
Markus Redmond:&nbsp; No….no…I’ve written
a lot of television.&nbsp;

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:06.910
I love television!&nbsp;

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.870
I watch more television than I watch films.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:10.870
Film Courage: Do you?&nbsp;

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:11.870
O.k…

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:12.870
Markus Redmond:&nbsp;&nbsp; I do.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.080
I love television.&nbsp;

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.780
I love Doctor Who…..ahhh…so good!&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.610
But no, I focus on feature film.&nbsp;

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:31.800
I think that is where my best work lives,
is in feature film.&nbsp;

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:41.739
And it’s also the best way I can express
myself creatively and so what I look for is

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:49.860
a ways and means to find funding/financing
for these small little films.&nbsp;

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.820
I’ve been fortunate enough to do them for
incredibly small budgets because I don’t

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:03.440
really have a lot of characters in my films
because they are just really simple stories

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.500
about people and their relationships.&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.360
It shouldn’t cost 20 million dollars to
tell a story about 4 people who are trying

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.210
to figure out life and that’s the kind of
stuff dig.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:23.030
Film Courage:&nbsp; So when you’re writing and
you’re trying to keep the idea of keeping

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.720
costs down, are there times when you’re
like ‘let me change that scene to one room

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.659
apartment versus someone’s office building’
because you’re thinking of cost?

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.350
Markus Redmond:&nbsp; I don’t do that.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:39.420
I just want to write story.&nbsp;

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:46.780
I think it can be hurtful to the story to
do that because you just want to tell a good

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.159
story.&nbsp;

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:53.840
Obviously if you’ve got things at your disposal,
you want to write toward those things (if

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.369
you can) and still tell a good story.&nbsp;

00:01:56.369 --> 00:02:02.869
I think story still has to be key because
if you’ve got an old abandon warehouse that

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:08.460
you can shoot in for nothing, that’s awesome!&nbsp;

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.169
But if you just shoot a crap story in an old
abandon warehouse because you have it for

00:02:13.169 --> 00:02:15.129
free, you’re going to have a crap movie.&nbsp;

00:02:15.129 --> 00:02:22.150
You still have to create a really good story.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:29.319
The piece that we’re working on now happen
mostly in one house.&nbsp;

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:36.459
But it’s a psychological thriller and there
are all sorts of things happening and characters

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:38.540
are turning and changing.&nbsp;

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:46.889
You don’t know who did what, to whom and
where, and why.&nbsp;

00:02:46.889 --> 00:02:53.450
As I was writing it and reading it back, it
just became a compelling story.&nbsp;

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:59.159
When you’re doing that, it ends up reading
like a play because you’re not moving around

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:00.719
to so many different locations.&nbsp;

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:08.319
But that wasn’t written to be in one location.&nbsp;

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:11.889
It was written and it happened to be in one
location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.889 --> 00:03:21.669
I would rather write a story first and then
compact for budget (or whatever) later.&nbsp;

00:03:21.669 --> 00:03:23.709
If that makes sense?

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:30.760
Film Courage:&nbsp; Do you think storytelling
is as prevalent and as rich as it once was?

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.760
Markus Redmond:&nbsp; No.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.180
Film Courage:&nbsp; I mean…(take the TV show
Taxi)…just think about the characters…

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.040
Markus Redmond:&nbsp;&nbsp; There are Taxi episodes
that are better than most movies.&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.019
You know what I mean?&nbsp;

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:46.829
Taxi probably had a profound effect on how
I write because it’s just so good!&nbsp;

00:03:46.829 --> 00:03:47.829
God…

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:52.900
Film Courage:&nbsp; And everyone is so flawed
in their own way, but they’re still likable…

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.939
Markus Redmond:&nbsp; But that’s what I’m
saying…those stories, those people, they

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:02.180
are just people in a cab garage and you don’t
really need to go to a whole lot of places

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.099
because the characters are so strong.&nbsp;

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:10.739
Listen, there are stories out there that are
fantastic and there are people out there making

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:22.550
wonderful films both big and small, but I
think we’ve become a very franchised, motivated

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:31.271
industry because a lot of money is being spent
on a lot of films and that money has got to

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:35.960
come from somewhere and it’s got to get
made back.&nbsp;

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:48.169
You can’t make 2 billion dollars off of
a movie that only cost 10 million dollars.

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:58.110
That’s all business and corporate strategy,
industry stuff, some of it has to do with

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:02.860
storytelling, some of it doesn’t.&nbsp;

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:13.009
I think we might be in a place where story
is going to become the only affordable, special

00:05:13.009 --> 00:05:14.590
effect here pretty soon.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:15.590
What do you think?&nbsp;

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:16.590
Are we getting better or worse at telling
stories?&nbsp;

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:16.601
